# Caddy in front of Miserly (copy to Caddyfile; see docker-compose.yml).
#
# Miserly gets a host name of its own, so its session cookie isn't shared with other
# apps on the same machine, and Caddy serves it over HTTPS.
#
# A name the public internet can resolve (a domain you own, pointed at this machine):
# Caddy fetches and renews a certificate by itself.
#
# miserly.example.com {
#     # After the first visit, browsers only open this name over HTTPS.
#     header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
#     reverse_proxy miserly:3000
# }
#
# A name only your home network knows (set in your router's DNS or each device's hosts
# file): Caddy signs the certificate with its own local authority. Each device that opens
# Miserly must trust that authority once (Caddy's docs: "tls internal", "caddy trust").
#
# miserly.home.example {
#     tls internal
#     # After the first visit, browsers only open this name over HTTPS.
#     header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
#     reverse_proxy miserly:3000
# }
#
# Either way, start Miserly with ORIGIN set to the same address, for example
# ORIGIN=https://miserly.home.example docker compose up -d
